# Design docs

## Locations

| Document | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `docs/design.ja.md` | Canonical design (Japanese). Requirements, threat model, formats, protocols, contracts, phases |
| `docs/design-decisions.md` | Rejected alternatives and why (Decision / Reason / Rejected) |
| `docs/adr/NNNN-*.md` | One decision per file whose reason is a measurement or an outside fact (Status, Date, Issue, Context, Decision, Consequences) |
| `docs/roadmap.md` | Rows: ID, title, Issue, status. No acceptance text |
| `docs/conventions.md` | Directory tree, project responsibilities, coding conventions |

`.dev/` is local scratch and is gitignored. Do not put lasting decisions there.

## Changing the design

- Requirement, contract, public API, wire or file format, threat model, or phase: open a `design` Issue, update `docs/design.ja.md` (and `docs/design-decisions.md` for what was rejected) in its PR, and merge it before implementation Issues.
- Wording, typos, and examples: may ship in the same PR as code.
- Do not implement first and update the design later. If code and design conflict, stop and fix the design through the process above.

## What a design statement must carry

- A claim about what another system or standard does (FlexNet, Keygen, JWS, an OS API) names the document page, RFC section, or source file and line where it was read, and is read there before it goes in. A rule taken from a specification (an RFC, a NIST publication) is copied from the section, read for the purpose, and the section is named.
- A format (license token, request, response) gives every field: its type, its encoding, whether it is signed, whether it is required, and what a reader does with an unknown field or an unknown version.
- A protocol gives every message, every error code, the order of the checks, and what each side does on a timeout, a retry, and a duplicate.
- A design that reads input from outside (a token file, a request body, a key file, an OS value) treats it as untrusted and writes its limits (size, depth, count) and what happens past them. A size the input claims is checked against what the input can hold before a buffer of that size is made.
- A security statement names the threat (who the attacker is, what they hold, what they want) and the property that stops it. A threat the design accepts is written as accepted, with the reason.
- A design that adds threads, timers, or OS resources writes their lifetime: who creates them, when they stop, who releases them, and what happens when they cannot be created.
- A design that uses a value read from the OS (a machine ID, the clock) says what it is read for, when it is read, when it is read again, and what happens when it cannot be read.
- A figure the design derives (a token size, a lease count, a memory bound) writes its arithmetic beside it.
- Every type in a signature the design writes is defined in the design or the code.
- A design that leaves a point to another row ("row X decides") names a row that exists in `docs/roadmap.md`, and that row's text names the point, in the same PR (`.cursor/rules/defer.mdc`).
- A design PR that changes a decision searches the whole design for the sections that restate it, and changes them in the same PR. Search by the decision's number (`0.xx`) and by its key words. In D0, decision 0.39 moved the certificate check to the token's `iat`, and §7.3 and T12 kept the old current-time rule until the owner's review found them (PR #4).

After a script edits a Markdown table, read `git diff --stat`. A diff of the whole table means its columns were padded again: restore the file and replace only the rows that change.

## Review before the PR

A design PR is reviewed by an independent reader before it is opened: an agent that reads only the documents and the rules, not the conversation that wrote them, and reports security holes, contradictions, dangling references, and rule violations. Each finding is fixed in the design or put to the owner as a question. The review's summary goes on the Issue as a comment, so that the PR body can cite its finding IDs. In D0 this review found 35 points in the first draft, among them a fake server minting leases from a trial license and a negative seat count (Issue #3).

## Judge the current optimum

The design records a decision made at that time. It is not proof that the decision is still the best one.

- Recommend the best solution for the problem as it stands now.
- Do not cite an existing sentence as the reason to keep that shape.
- Do not add public API only so an older shape can stay.
- If the written decision is no longer the best one, say so and change the design through the process above.
